\section*{Log-squared sharp threshold for monotone graph properties}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
\textit{Sharp thresholds for graph properties}: The claimed threshold interval for a vertex-invariant increasing graph property has width at most a universal multiple of $\log(1/(2\varepsilon))/(\log n)^2$. This comparison uses the probabilities $\varepsilon$ and $1-\varepsilon$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} Let $\mathcal P$ be a nontrivial increasing property of simple graphs on $n\ge2$ vertices, unchanged when vertex labels are permuted. Write $p_a=\inf\{p:\mathbb P(G(n,p)\in\mathcal P)\ge a\}$. For every $0<\epsilon<1/2$ the claimed sharp-threshold bound is \[p_{1-\epsilon}-p_\epsilon\le\frac{2^{19}}{(\log n)^2}\log\frac1{2\epsilon}.\] Logs are natural and $n$ counts vertices rather than potential edges.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
\textit{Sharp thresholds for graph properties}: A random block of vertices is retained while all edges disjoint from it are conditioned upon, preserving a useful permutation symmetry. A biased Fourier estimate bounds conditional low-degree mass linearly in influence. A small-degree vertex in each indexing graph transfers that bound to a larger original degree range, and integration of the probability derivative gives the quantile width.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
\textit{Sharp thresholds for graph properties}: The graph paper proves a uniform log-square sharp-threshold width; the separate hypergraph paper proves Var\_p(f)<=C\_r I\_p(f)/(log n)\textasciicircum{}(r/(r-1)) for every relabeling-invariant r-uniform hypergraph property, r>=3.

With the hypotheses and conventions above, the claimed result addresses the whole mathematical assertion on this page.

\subsection{Formal verification}
Family 186 describes Lean coverage for the graph-property threshold width; the hypergraph refinement is not part of this selection.

\textit{Sharp thresholds for graph properties}: The manuscript links \texttt{SharpThreshold.lean}.

The documented selection includes this manuscript’s main assertion; that assertion retains the restrictions stated above.

This site has not run the Lean development. These links describe OpenAI’s selected formal statements and should be read with their stated scope.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI reports that an internal model produced the manuscripts. Their arguments have not been independently verified as proofs by this site. Any portion outside the documented formal scope may contain mistakes, and the catalogue label reports the announced mathematical claim.

